  "abstract": "Common-face gluing is a natural way to preserve two planar layers, but its coloring constraints can prevent a construction from raising the chromatic number. We prove that every proper nine-precoloring of a common-cofacial boundary extends across each of the ten directed first-generation Hajós joins of two copies of the classical Sulanke graph, and across a previously certified seventeen-vertex, 82-edge core. Both statements hold uniformly over every planar-layer representation, and allow repeated boundary colors. Consequently, these graphs are reducible induced configurations whenever all exterior attachments lie on such a boundary; no ten-critical biplanar graph can contain an attachment of this form. For the Hajós family, triangulation allocation, shared-star contraction and face density reduce the proof to complete half-color partitions and exact palette matching. A standard-library verifier checks 7,203,560 proper boundary partitions, with a separate full-graph SAT cross-check. For the seventeen-vertex core, complement matching leaves 49 algebraic failures among 243,865 precolorings. Density excludes 32, while a two-chord contraction excludes the remaining seventeen through a single six-vertex pattern. This yields a general boundary obstruction for biplanar graphs with at least $6n-20$ edges, supported by a six-row cyclic-order certificate. The results strengthen earlier rainbow-only gluing certificates to arbitrary precoloring extension. They do not cover interfaces spread over several faces, additional edges inside the configurations, or arbitrary iterated Hajós joins, and do not resolve the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem.",
